WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:03.490
Imagine a supervisor who wants to hand someone the lead

2
00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:07.030
on a community listening session. Two colleagues are interested. One

3
00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:09.810
is a trusted colleague of many years. The other is

4
00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.610
newer, and comes from the community the session is meant

5
00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:12.970
to reach.

6
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.420
That decision looks small, but it says a lot about

7
00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:18.880
how trust works on a team. So what does the

8
00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.300
page mean by empowering, or delegative, leadership?

9
00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:26.010
It means handing over real decision making authority, with clear

10
00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:30.550
goals, resources and support. The leader sets the destination and

11
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:34.470
the limits, such as budget, legal requirements, deadlines and quality,

12
00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:37.630
makes sure the person has the authority and tools they

13
00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:40.650
need, and then steps back from managing every step of

14
00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:40.930
the path.

15
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.900
How is that different from a leader who simply leaves

16
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.480
people alone? From the outside, both seem hands off.

17
00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:51.310
That is the key distinction. Laissez-faire leadership is stepping back

18
00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:55.390
without clarity or support. Empowering leadership is structured and actively

19
00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:59.710
supported. The leader stays involved in alignment, coordination and coaching

20
00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:02.510
when asked, and gives people cover so they can make

21
00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.370
real decisions without being second guessed.

22
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.420
What would I notice day to day with an empowering

23
00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:11.180
leader? What makes delegation feel like genuine trust rather than

24
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:12.680
just more work landing on my desk?

25
00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.990
You would be given whole, meaningful pieces of work, not

26
00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.710
only the tedious tasks. You would know exactly what you

27
00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.350
can decide alone, what needs a quick heads up, and

28
00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.390
what needs sign off. The leader would back your authority

29
00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:28.910
in front of others. Check-ins would happen at agreed milestones,

30
00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:30.010
not hourly.

31
00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.930
And when a reasonable call falls short, it becomes a

32
00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:36.910
chance to learn, not a reason to take the authority

33
00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:37.290
back.

34
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.980
Where does this style work best? It sounds especially valuable

35
00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:45.640
in complex organizations where no single leader can know everything.

36
00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:52.090
Exactly there. With experienced, motivated professionals, delegating decisions removes approval

37
00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:55.470
bottlenecks, speeds up responses to the public and builds pride

38
00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:59.330
in the work. Over time, gradually widening people's decision rights

39
00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:01.610
builds a bench of colleagues who can step into larger

40
00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:02.830
roles when turnover comes.

41
00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.480
And where does it go wrong? I imagine handing over

42
00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:09.060
authority too early could feel more like being abandoned than

43
00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:09.260
empowered.

44
00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:13.830
Delegation only empowers when the person is ready and resourced.

45
00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:17.530
Handing a high stakes, unclear project to someone brand new,

46
00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:21.410
without training or boundaries, sets them up to struggle. Delegating

47
00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:25.370
responsibility without authority, so people must deliver results while asking

48
00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:28.150
permission for every small thing, is deeply frustrating.

49
00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:32.090
And giving the best opportunities only to a small inner

50
00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:35.530
circle burns that circle out while everyone else disengages.

51
00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.000
The page says this style can accelerate equity or amplify

52
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.240
bias, depending on who gets empowered. That seems like the

53
00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:44.160
crux of the whole conversation.

54
00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:49.350
It is. Fairly distributed authority directly builds the track record

55
00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:52.730
people need for promotion, which helps close gaps in who

56
00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:56.290
reaches leadership. It lets front line staff with lived experience

57
00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:58.610
adapt how they serve people in real time.

58
00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:03.390
And focusing on outcomes rather than methods gives neurodivergent colleagues,

59
00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:06.850
disabled colleagues and caregivers flexibility to work in the way

60
00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:07.690
that suits them best.

61
00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:11.360
And the risks? I suspect they relate to whom we

62
00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.580
naturally trust first, without really noticing that we are doing

63
00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:14.780
it.

64
00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:19.410
Yes. People tend to extend trust faster to those who

65
00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.770
resemble them, and apply closer scrutiny to others with the

66
00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:28.150
same qualifications. When that happens, empowerment itself becomes unequal. The

67
00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:32.350
page also describes the glass cliff: giving underrepresented staff risky,

68
00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:36.470
underfunded projects without backing, so they absorb the blame when

69
00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:37.310
the project struggles.

70
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.880
So back to the listening session. What should the supervisor

71
00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.180
do with the two interested colleagues?

72
00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.850
Offer the lead to the newer colleague, with clear goals,

73
00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:50.070
resources and a check-in, and invite the experienced colleague to

74
00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:53.070
support them. Real authority with real support develops a new

75
00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:56.370
leader and brings community connection into the room. Handing it

76
00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:59.010
over and stepping away entirely would leave the newer colleague

77
00:03:59.010 --> 00:03:59.330
exposed.

78
00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:04.040
That answer shows trust and support working together rather than

79
00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.920
against each other. What else does the page suggest leaders

80
00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:07.280
try?

81
00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:12.550
Regularly review who receives high visibility, independent assignments and who

82
00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:16.030
receives routine tasks. When you empower someone to lead sensitive

83
00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:19.610
or cross unit work, affirm their authority publicly and make

84
00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:22.050
sure they have the budget, time and backing to succeed.

85
00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:25.370
And ask every person what context and check-ins they need,

86
00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:27.710
rather than assuming based on identity or background.

87
00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:31.640
Here is a question to carry with you. Who do

88
00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.500
you trust with real decisions, and why them? Thank you

89
00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:34.920
for listening.
